WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.759
Some Cloud.md directives change what Cloud Code does,

2
00:00:03.759 --> 00:00:06.119
and some just sit there and get read.

3
00:00:06.119 --> 00:00:08.520
The difference is mostly craft.

4
00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:12.279
In this lesson, we'll work through what makes an instruction land,

5
00:00:12.279 --> 00:00:16.520
which repo conventions and process rules are worth encoding,

6
00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:19.040
what to leave out because the model already knows it,

7
00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:22.840
and how to structure the file so it stays usable.

8
00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:26.600
The difference between an instruction Cloud Code follows,

9
00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:30.200
and one it skips usually comes down to precision,

10
00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:33.080
and precision has a cost in context.

11
00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:37.599
An effective instruction is specific, actionable, and unambiguous.

12
00:00:37.599 --> 00:00:41.799
Cloud Code can follow it without inferring intent or filling gaps.

13
00:00:41.799 --> 00:00:44.080
A vague directive may be read,

14
00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:46.099
but not reliably acted on.

15
00:00:46.099 --> 00:00:51.819
Context window cost is the trade-off at the center of Cloud.md design.

16
00:00:51.819 --> 00:00:54.560
Every token you put in the file counts against

17
00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:57.599
available context space during the session.

18
00:00:57.599 --> 00:01:01.599
Verbose files leave less room for code and tool output,

19
00:01:01.599 --> 00:01:05.519
and instruction encoding is the decision about what belongs in

20
00:01:05.519 --> 00:01:10.919
Cloud.md versus what Cloud Code already knows from training.

21
00:01:10.919 --> 00:01:13.559
General programming knowledge doesn't belong in

22
00:01:13.559 --> 00:01:17.180
Cloud.md repo specific decisions do.

23
00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:19.199
That distinction drives most of

24
00:01:19.199 --> 00:01:22.279
the design choices we'll cover in this lesson.

25
00:01:22.279 --> 00:01:27.080
Three traits define an effective Cloud.md directive.

26
00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:29.839
Specific means it names the exact language version,

27
00:01:29.839 --> 00:01:31.199
the precise file path,

28
00:01:31.199 --> 00:01:33.040
the actual naming convention.

29
00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:35.239
Not use consistent naming,

30
00:01:35.239 --> 00:01:38.160
but all module files use snake case.

31
00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:40.199
Actionable means Cloud Code can

32
00:01:40.199 --> 00:01:42.959
follow the instruction without interpretation.

33
00:01:42.959 --> 00:01:45.400
If following it requires a judgment call,

34
00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:47.760
the instruction isn't actionable enough.

35
00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:50.559
Unambiguous means one clear meaning.

36
00:01:50.639 --> 00:01:53.839
No sentence that could reasonably be read two ways.

37
00:01:53.839 --> 00:01:57.000
Vague instructions produce inconsistent behavior.

38
00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:58.879
Cloud Code may read them,

39
00:01:58.879 --> 00:02:02.459
but the behavior you see in practice will vary across sessions.

40
00:02:02.459 --> 00:02:04.160
The standard is precision.

41
00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:07.160
If you can't verify whether the instruction was followed,

42
00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:09.399
rewrite it until you can.

43
00:02:09.399 --> 00:02:13.080
Cloud Code reads both concise bullet directives and

44
00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:16.000
verbose documentation, but concise directives

45
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:18.399
are more reliably followed in practice.

46
00:02:18.559 --> 00:02:21.759
A short bullet point stating a specific rule is harder to

47
00:02:21.759 --> 00:02:23.720
miss than the same rule buried

48
00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:26.559
three paragraphs into a design rationale.

49
00:02:26.559 --> 00:02:30.160
Verbose documentation also consumes context space.

50
00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:33.320
Every sentence of narrative that doesn't add a new directive

51
00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:36.880
is a sentence that could have been code or conversation.

52
00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:38.800
The right mental model for writing

53
00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:41.679
Cloud.md is a style guide checklist,

54
00:02:41.679 --> 00:02:43.160
not a design document.

55
00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:45.919
Short bullets, one rule per line,

56
00:02:45.919 --> 00:02:47.699
no explanatory paragraphs,

57
00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:51.259
unless the instruction can't be understood without context.

58
00:02:51.259 --> 00:02:52.660
When in doubt, cut.

59
00:02:53.679 --> 00:02:57.020
Four categories belong in almost every production,

60
00:02:57.020 --> 00:03:00.460
Cloud.md language and runtime versions.

61
00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:04.179
Python 3.11, Node 20 LTS,

62
00:03:04.179 --> 00:03:07.240
whatever exact versions this project requires.

63
00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:09.899
Cloud Code won't assume a specific version

64
00:03:09.899 --> 00:03:11.500
without being told.

65
00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:14.059
Style conventions, the naming patterns,

66
00:03:14.059 --> 00:03:17.419
import order, and file organization decisions,

67
00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:21.179
your team has made for this repo specifically.

68
00:03:21.179 --> 00:03:23.660
Test requirements, coverage thresholds,

69
00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:25.580
test file naming conventions,

70
00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:29.220
fixture organization, and any policies around mocking.

71
00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:31.759
And commit format, the message structure,

72
00:03:31.759 --> 00:03:33.220
branch naming pattern,

73
00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:36.460
and PR description template your team uses.

74
00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:39.020
These are all repo specific decisions.

75
00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:40.500
They're not universal.

76
00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:43.500
Cloud Code can't know them from training data.

77
00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:46.440
That's exactly why they belong in Cloud.md.

78
00:03:47.419 --> 00:03:48.619
Beyond conventions,

79
00:03:48.619 --> 00:03:51.979
Cloud.md is where you document team process

80
00:03:51.979 --> 00:03:54.500
and known anti-patterns.

81
00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:57.300
PR process rules should be explicit.

82
00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:00.399
Who needs to approve, what the merge strategy is,

83
00:04:00.399 --> 00:04:02.300
whether commits are squashed.

84
00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:05.059
Coding anti-patterns specific to this code base

85
00:04:05.059 --> 00:04:06.399
are worth encoding.

86
00:04:06.399 --> 00:04:08.940
Patterns that have caused problems before,

87
00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:11.220
architectural decisions to avoid,

88
00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:13.619
common mistakes that show up in review.

89
00:04:13.619 --> 00:04:16.260
And security rules belong here too.

90
00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:17.959
No hard-coded credentials,

91
00:04:17.959 --> 00:04:20.100
which files are never to be modified,

92
00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:21.700
how secrets are handled.

93
00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:24.299
These are decisions your team has already made

94
00:04:24.299 --> 00:04:26.660
through experience and deliberation.

95
00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:30.179
Encoding them in Cloud.md means Cloud Code

96
00:04:30.179 --> 00:04:33.320
won't re-suggest the patterns you've already ruled out.

97
00:04:34.339 --> 00:04:38.500
What not to encode is as important as what to encode.

98
00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:41.700
Cloud.md is for repo specific knowledge,

99
00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:44.220
not general programming education.

100
00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:46.019
Don't explain what Python is,

101
00:04:46.779 --> 00:04:48.820
or document how list comprehensions work.

102
00:04:48.820 --> 00:04:51.440
Don't restate standard library behavior

103
00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:54.299
that Cloud Code knows thoroughly from training.

104
00:04:54.299 --> 00:04:57.459
Don't duplicate rules that your linter or formatter

105
00:04:57.459 --> 00:04:59.000
already enforces.

106
00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:00.779
If black handles your formatting,

107
00:05:00.779 --> 00:05:04.739
you don't need formatting rules in Cloud.md.

108
00:05:04.739 --> 00:05:07.380
If ESLint catches an import pattern,

109
00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:09.839
don't also encode it as a directive.

110
00:05:09.839 --> 00:05:12.859
Duplicating a tool creates a second source of truth

111
00:05:12.859 --> 00:05:14.899
that eventually drifts out of sync

112
00:05:14.899 --> 00:05:16.899
with the tool's own config.

113
00:05:16.899 --> 00:05:20.660
Let tools enforce what tools can enforce.

114
00:05:20.660 --> 00:05:23.959
Every line of general knowledge wastes context tokens

115
00:05:23.959 --> 00:05:26.119
that could hold actual code output.

116
00:05:27.140 --> 00:05:31.339
The encode versus skip decision comes down to one question.

117
00:05:31.339 --> 00:05:35.299
Is this something Cloud Code can only know if you tell it?

118
00:05:35.299 --> 00:05:38.160
Runtime version pins are a clear yes.

119
00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:40.500
Cloud Code doesn't know which Python version

120
00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:43.420
your team is targeting without being told.

121
00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:46.459
Your PR naming convention is a clear yes.

122
00:05:46.459 --> 00:05:49.579
Your team's deployment process is a clear yes.

123
00:05:49.579 --> 00:05:52.980
General Python syntax is a clear no.

124
00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:55.779
Cloud Code knows how Python works.

125
00:05:55.779 --> 00:05:58.779
Standard design patterns are a clear no.

126
00:05:58.779 --> 00:06:02.019
How Git branching works is a clear no.

127
00:06:02.019 --> 00:06:05.119
The practical test is, if I removed this line,

128
00:06:05.119 --> 00:06:08.660
would Cloud Code behave differently in a way that matters?

129
00:06:08.660 --> 00:06:11.880
If yes, keep it. If no, cut it.

130
00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:16.079
That filter keeps Cloud.md focused and context efficient.

131
00:06:17.140 --> 00:06:19.679
Structure matters as much as content.

132
00:06:19.679 --> 00:06:22.260
A Cloud.md with clear header sections

133
00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:24.959
is faster for Cloud Code to process

134
00:06:24.959 --> 00:06:28.359
and easier for you to verify and maintain.

135
00:06:28.359 --> 00:06:31.299
Group related rules together, a language section,

136
00:06:31.299 --> 00:06:33.399
a style section, a testing section,

137
00:06:33.399 --> 00:06:35.980
a Git section, a security section.

138
00:06:35.980 --> 00:06:38.559
Use bullet points over paragraphs.

139
00:06:38.559 --> 00:06:40.299
One directive per line.

140
00:06:40.299 --> 00:06:42.380
Related rules in the same section,

141
00:06:42.380 --> 00:06:45.980
so context pressure hits a whole category at once

142
00:06:45.980 --> 00:06:47.760
rather than a mixed list.

143
00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:50.279
Good structure also makes testing easier.

144
00:06:50.279 --> 00:06:54.040
When you sit down to verify that your Cloud.md works,

145
00:06:54.040 --> 00:06:56.559
you can work through it section by section

146
00:06:56.559 --> 00:06:58.720
instead of hunting for scattered rules

147
00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:00.540
across a long document.

148
00:07:00.540 --> 00:07:03.179
Headers and bullets are both a readability choice

149
00:07:03.179 --> 00:07:05.040
and a reliability choice.

150
00:07:06.019 --> 00:07:09.480
Think of that file as a standing tax on every session.

151
00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:12.059
Cloud Code reads it before it reads your code.

152
00:07:12.059 --> 00:07:14.140
So a line restating general knowledge

153
00:07:14.140 --> 00:07:18.619
costs exactly as much as a line that saves the afternoon.

154
00:07:18.619 --> 00:07:21.980
Specificity is what earns a directive its place.

155
00:07:21.980 --> 00:07:24.220
And repo conventions and process rules

156
00:07:24.220 --> 00:07:27.859
are the ones no training run could have supplied.

157
00:07:27.859 --> 00:07:29.420
You'll only know a directive works

158
00:07:29.420 --> 00:07:33.059
by watching a real session and seeing how it behaves.

159
00:07:33.059 --> 00:07:34.540
And that's it for this lesson.

160
00:07:34.540 --> 00:07:35.980
I'll see you in the next one.

